  "id": "Compound::DB00786 - Gene::4320",
  "verbalization": "Marimastat, a small molecule inhibitor, is shown to bind to multiple matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) through the visible paths, including Mmp23b, Mmp26, and Mmp27. These MMPs are all part of the same functional group, participating in the degradation of extracellular matrix components. The shared participation in MMPs by Mmp11 suggests a potential role for these MMPs in a common biological process, such as tissue remodeling or inflammation. The consistent binding of Marimastat to these MMPs, along with their shared participation in the same pathway, supports a mechanistic link between Marimastat and Mmp11. The final scores across the three paths indicate a strong, reliable interaction, which is further supported by the shared functional role of the MMPs in the pathway. The evidence suggests that Marimastat may inhibit Mmp11 through its interaction with these MMPs, potentially affecting the activity of Mmp11 in a cellular context.",
